---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CONFIG } from '../lib/hero-stream';
const tokens = [
  ['--bg', 'Page paper'], ['--surface', 'Cards'], ['--ink', 'Text'], ['--muted', 'Secondary text'], ['--line', 'Rules'], ['--accent', 'Signal'],
];
---
<BaseLayout title="How this site was built" description="Design decisions, tokens, motion principles, performance budget and accessibility choices behind this portfolio.">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Meta case study</p>
      <h1 style="margin:.5rem 0 1.5rem;max-width:18ch">How this site was built</h1>
      <p class="tldr">A static Astro site where every project is one Markdown file, the design is a small set of tokens, and motion is only used where it explains something. This page is the reasoning.</p>

      <div class="cs-section"><h2>Concept: Blueprint</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>The site is drawn like an engineering drawing: warm paper in light mode, a drafting table at night in dark mode, hairline rules, square corners and one signal-orange accent. It matches the work, which is systems with rails, queues and packets, and it avoids the usual card-grid template look.</p>
          <p>Type is a pair: <b>Martian Mono</b> for headings and labels, <b>IBM Plex Sans</b> for reading. Both are self-hosted, Latin subset only, with <code>font-display: swap</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-section"><h2>Design tokens</h2>
        <p class="prose">Colors, spacing, radius, shadow and motion are CSS variables, used everywhere. These swatches are read live from the current theme, so toggle the theme and watch them change.</p>
        <div class="metrics" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
          {tokens.map(([v, label]) => (
            <div><div style={`height:3.5rem;background:var(${v});border:1px solid var(--line)`}></div><p class="mono" style="font-size:.78rem;margin:.5rem 0 0">{v}</p><span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem">{label}</span></div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="prose" style="margin-top:1.5rem">Each project also carries its own accent color (light and dark), resolved per theme, so a case study feels like its own while staying inside the system.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-section"><h2>Motion principles</h2>
        <ul class="prose">
          <li><b>Motion explains or guides.</b> Nothing moves just because it can.</li>
          <li><b>Short and consistent.</b> UI transitions are 150 to 400 ms; hero and storytelling sequences up to about 1.2 s. They use shared easing tokens.</li>
          <li><b>Transform and opacity only</b>, so animation never triggers layout.</li>
          <li><b>Calm by default.</b> The hero stream is {CONFIG.particles} faint packets, each crossing in {CONFIG.crossSeconds[0]} to {CONFIG.crossSeconds[1]} seconds. It pauses off-screen and in hidden tabs.</li>
          <li><b>Content is never hidden behind an animation.</b> With JavaScript off or reduced motion on, everything is simply visible.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:1.5rem"><button class="btn" type="button" id="ease-demo">Try the standard ease-out</button></p>
        <div style="height:2.75rem;border:1px solid var(--line);position:relative;overflow:hidden;max-width:28rem">
          <div id="ease-dot" style="position:absolute;left:0;top:.9rem;width:.9rem;height:.9rem;background:var(--accent)"></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-section"><h2>Accessibility</h2>
        <ul class="prose">
          <li>Semantic landmarks, a skip link, visible focus on every interactive element, and keyboard access to every control.</li>
          <li>Text and accent colors are chosen for at least 4.5:1 contrast in both themes.</li>
          <li>The theme follows the system and the toggle persists, with storage wrapped in try/catch.</li>
          <li>Hover effects have touch-friendly equivalents, and touch targets are at least 44 px.</li>
          <li>The project filter, diagrams and hero all degrade to plain, readable content without JavaScript.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-section"><h2>Performance budget</h2>
        <p class="prose">Targets: LCP under 2.0 s on a mid-range phone, CLS under 0.05, initial JavaScript under 100 KB gzipped, Lighthouse at least 90 for Performance and 95 for Accessibility, Best Practices and SEO. The hero canvas loads after the page is idle, and is skipped on low-power devices.</p>
        <p class="prose">Measured with Lighthouse in mobile mode (simulated slow 4G, 4x CPU slowdown) against the production build, on all eight pages:</p>
        <div class="metrics" style="margin-top:1rem">
          <div class="metric"><b>99–100</b><span>Performance</span></div>
          <div class="metric"><b>100</b><span>Accessibility, Best Practices and SEO</span></div>
          <div class="metric"><b>1.4–1.7 s</b><span>Largest Contentful Paint</span></div>
          <div class="metric"><b>0.000</b><span>Cumulative Layout Shift</span></div>
          <div class="metric"><b>13–15 KB</b><span>JavaScript on most pages; 57 KB on case studies, where the diagram animation loads</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem;font-size:.9rem;color:var(--muted)">Run locally against <code>astro preview</code>, so real-world numbers on GitHub Pages may differ slightly. The same budgets are enforced in CI with Lighthouse CI.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-section"><h2>Content model</h2>
        <p class="prose">Each project is one MDX file with Zod-validated frontmatter, plus one SVG diagram whose elements carry <code>data-step</code> attributes. Adding a project never requires editing a page component. The site is static, has no backend and no analytics.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
<script>
  import { reducedMotion } from '../lib/ui';
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', () => {
  const btn = document.getElementById('ease-demo');
  const dot = document.getElementById('ease-dot');
  let right = false;
  btn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
    if (!dot) return;
    right = !right;
    const reduce = reducedMotion();
    dot.style.transition = reduce ? 'none' : 'transform 400ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)';
    dot.style.transform = right ? `translateX(${dot.parentElement!.clientWidth - dot.clientWidth}px)` : 'translateX(0)';
  });
  });
</script>
